      "problem": "Given that $‖u‖=3$ and $u\\cdot v=4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖v‖.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |4| & \\le 3\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le 3\\cdot ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\frac{1}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 4 & \\le \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & \\le ‖v‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖v‖$\nis  $\\frac{4}{3}.$",
